     "text": "The design is complete and verified. Final report:\n\n## 1. What was created and drawn\n\n- **Alignment `ELM-AVE`** (design model): BEGIN [E 1000.00, N 3000.00] at 10+00.00; PI-1 [1787.85, 2861.08] (Δ=40°00'00\" L, R=500, T=181.99, L=349.07, BC 16+18.01, EC 19+67.08); PI-2 [2394.06, 3211.08] (Δ=25°00'00\" R, R=700, T=155.19, L=305.43, BC 23+29.91, EC 26+35.34); END [2991.78, 3263.37] at 30+80.16. Total length 2080.16 ft. Bearings S 80°E → N 60°E → N 85°E.\n- **Centerline** on `align_MAIN`: LN-001, AR-001, LN-002, AR-002, LN-003 (anchored to the alignment).\n- **Edges of pavement** on `rd_EP`: offsets 11' LT / 11' RT (two 11-ft lanes) — LN-004…LN-009, AR-003…AR-006.\n- **Outside edges of shoulder** on `rd_ES`: offsets 16' LT / 16' RT (11 + 5 ft shoulder) — LN-010…LN-015, AR-007…AR-010.\n- **Stationing** on `rd_LAYOUT-NOTE-anno`: 21 ticks every 100 ft (10+00 to 30+00), station labels, and 4 BC/EC labels.\n\n## 2. Tool calls that mattered, in order\n\n1. `get_document_state` — empty model; layers align_MAIN, rd_EP, rd_ES, rd_LAYOUT-NOTE-anno already existed.\n2. `create_alignment` name=ELM-AVE, begin_station=\"10+00\", start=[1000, 3000], legs=[{bearing \"S 80°00'00\\\" E\", 800}, {deflection \"40 L\", radius 500, 700}, {deflection \"25 R\", radius 700, 600}].\n3. `draw_alignment` alignment=ELM-AVE, layer=align_MAIN.\n4. `draw_offset` alignment=ELM-AVE, offsets=[-11, 11], layer=rd_EP.\n5. `draw_offset` alignment=ELM-AVE, offsets=[-16, 16], layer=rd_ES.\n6. `annotate_stationing` space=model, alignment=ELM-AVE, interval=100, layer=rd_LAYOUT-NOTE-anno.\n7. `get_design_data` kind=curves + `render_model` — to verify curve data and look at the result.\n\n## 3. Problems / workarounds\n\nNone — no errors or warnings from any command. The only arithmetic I did myself was the offset distances: lane edge = 11 ft each side; shoulder edge = 11 + 5 = 16 ft each side. All coordinates (PIs, PC/PT points, curve centers) were computed by the engine from the bearing/distance/deflection description; N 3000 / E 1000 was entered as [x=1000, y=3000]."
